# Dynamic Rule Maintenance Protocol

After every meaningful code change, keep project-specific knowledge synchronized automatically — never wait for the user to ask: create missing rule folders/files, update existing rules, split oversized rules, refresh the project index, and remove references made stale by renames or deletions.

## Required structure — create anything missing
- `.cursor/rules/`: `00-core/`, `10-backend/`, `20-frontend/`, `30-database/`, `40-routes-api/`, `50-security/`, `60-domain/`, `70-workflows/`, `80-testing/`, `90-project/`
- `.ai/project-index/`: `PROJECT_MAP.md`, `ROUTES.md`, `FILES.md`, `RELATIONS.md`, `SYMBOLS.jsonl`, `manifest.json`, `DOMAINS/`

## Dynamic rule discovery — no hardcoded mapping table
After code changes: inspect changed files and detected symbols → determine which architectural concepts were affected → update the smallest existing rule files whose scope matches → create a focused new `.mdc` in the most appropriate category when none match → update or remove sections that no longer represent the codebase → fix all mappings and rule references after renames or deletions.

Discoverable concepts (examples, not fixed conditions): backend architecture; services/actions/handlers; controllers and requests; models and relationships; routes and middleware; APIs and resources; frontend pages/components/hooks; permissions and workflows; queues/events/listeners; security boundaries; database schema and indexes; tests and fixtures; domain-specific business rules.

## Rule file design
Each rule: one focused concern; exact file paths and symbol names where useful; verified facts separated from inferred conventions; concise; no source-code copies or file-inventory dumps; durable project knowledge only. Preferred max ~80 lines (~120 only when splitting would reduce clarity). When a rule outgrows a focused scope: split by domain/module/concern and keep a short parent/index rule linking to the children.

## Large projects and monorepos
Create rules per domain or bounded context (subfolders under `60-domain/`), per route group or application (`40-routes-api/groups/`), per backend module (`10-backend/modules/`), per frontend app/package (`20-frontend/apps/`), per schema or bounded context (`30-database/domains/`), and per major business flow (`70-workflows/`). Never put the entire project into one rule file.

## Synchronization procedure
1. Complete the code change.
2. Refresh the project index.
3. Read the changed file mappings and affected relationships.
4. Update or create the relevant rules.
5. Remove stale references.
6. Split any oversized rule.
7. Verify all referenced paths and symbols exist.
8. Finish only when rules and index match the codebase.

## Trivial change exception
Skip rule updates for typo-, whitespace-, or formatting-only changes, comments, temporary debug code, and purely cosmetic UI edits with no reusable convention or structural effect — but never delete or corrupt the existing index.
